<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Mohnyin</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1604 CE (approx.) · capital Mohnyin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A northern Shan state on the upper Irrawaddy whose saophas raided and finally conquered the Ava Kingdom in the 16th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mohnyin, Shan Mong Yang, lay in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irrawaddy-river">Irrawaddy</a> basin in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-state">Kachin State</a>. It emerged as a Shan principality in the 14th century, within the sphere of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mong-mao">Mong Mao</a>, and was later recognised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming China</a> as a pacification commission called Mengyang.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its saophas ruled a mix of Shan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-people">Kachin</a> and Burman subjects and were among the most aggressive of the Shan rulers. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawlon">Sawlon</a> in the early 16th century Mohnyin led a coalition of Shan states against Ava, and in 1527 they took Ava, killing its king. Sawlon's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thohanbwa">Thohanbwa</a> then reigned at Ava, notorious for attacking monasteries.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Shan hold on Ava was broken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a>, who conquered Mohnyin and the other northern Shan states in the 1550s. Mohnyin later lost its hereditary rulers and became part of the Burmese provincial order; the town survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachin-state">Kachin State</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1300 CE – 1604 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mainland Southeast Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.57%;width:6.05%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1604 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mohnyin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sawlon, Thohanbwa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism, Tai spirit worship</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mong Yang, Mohnyin State</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1452&amp;focus=mohnyin" aria-label="Mohnyin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7238 1265 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mohnyin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7558 1521L7609 1732"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7609 1732h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7558 1521h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7558 1521h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1452&amp;focus=mohnyin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
